data.content:
The lightest option depends on your target framework:

### .NET 8+ → Use the built-in `TimeProvider`

No new interfaces needed. Accept `TimeProvider` as a constructor parameter with a default of `TimeProvider.System`:

```csharp
namespace InvoiceLib;

public class InvoiceCalculator
{
    private readonly TimeProvider _time;

    public InvoiceCalculator(TimeProvider? timeProvider = null)
        => _time = timeProvider ?? TimeProvider.System;

    public bool IsOverdue(DateTimeOffset dueDate)
        => _time.GetUtcNow() > dueDate;
}
```

In tests, use `Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing` (a single NuGet package):

```csharp
var fake = new FakeTimeProvider(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 15, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(fake);

Assert.True(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 10, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
fake.Advance(TimeSpan.FromDays(-10)); // can rewind too
```

Production callers keep using `new InvoiceCalculator()` — no DI container required.

---

### Pre-.NET 8 → Thin `IClock` interface

```csharp
public interface IClock { DateTimeOffset UtcNow { get; } }
public sealed class SystemClock : IClock { public DateTimeOffset UtcNow => DateTimeOffset.UtcNow; }
```

Same constructor-default pattern: `IClock? clock = null` → fall back to `SystemClock`.

---

**Recommendation:** If you're on .NET 8+, `TimeProvider` is the standard answer — zero custom abstractions, first-party test fakes, and broad ecosystem support. Want me to apply this refactoring to your `InvoiceCalculator`?
